{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789968811,"nonce":"PhDAm3nXZM6imR3It9Iy0dkNo1c=","message":"Split of one promotion's install with ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1 (66dcee3701b2a0483c74cd29de52646ff4349c01 prints it), Mac arm64, Lua mandelbrot, one-function modules:\n\n    before: ir 3.6 to 4.1, opt 1.1 to 1.5, engine 0.07, codegen 2.6 to 4.2 ms (8.5 to 10 ms each)\n    after:  ir 0.05 to 0.14, opt 0.7 to 1.1, engine 0.07, codegen 2.4 to 3.5 ms (3.5 to 4.9 ms each)\n\nThe 4 ms of \"ir\" was ours: the promotion's module carried every global of the program (each declared and bound in LLVM IR) and the whole module was rendered to a string after lowering. The module now carries the globals its functions name, and the JIT path lowers without printing.\n\nWhat is left per module is LLVM's: opt is the pass pipeline built per run_passes, codegen is the per-module codegen pass manager plus RuntimeDyld. Engine creation, and with it MCJIT's per-engine TargetMachine, is 0.07 ms, so one TargetMachine per process buys nothing there; -misched-regpressure=false (via ZYNTAX_LLVM_ARGS) left codegen at 3.3 ms either way. The first promotion of a process still pays about 7 ms more than the steady state (opt 4.7, codegen 5.3); a dummy module compiled ahead in a context and target machine of its own did not remove it (measured, then dropped), so that cost is per context or per target machine, not per process. Batching hot functions into one module, as proposed here, remains the way to amortise the 3.5 ms.","files":null}]}